From 4ad746932bf083188109b615cb9be7f2306f159a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 19:33:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] M14: instruction fetch stops at an ATC fault, leaving the
 next page alone

The fetch unit queued a faulted fetch's words marked (q_atc) and fetched
on: a fault near the end of a page went on to the next page, searched the
tables for it and, that page invalid too, made its ATC entry not resident
(UM 3.2.2.3). Linux's handler maps the faulting page and, by fault-around,
the next ones, and flushes the faulting page's ATC entry only (the PTEST
in access_error040): the fetch after its RTE hit the stale entry and
faulted again, and Linux, whose PTEST now found the page resident, took
it for a protection violation. Found by litex-68k running buildroot's
glibc userspace on RD68041 in a LiteX SoC: init's first fopen64, at the
end of a page, ran into the next with a MOVE.L across the two; init died
of SIGSEGV and the kernel panicked.

The fetch unit now stops after an ATC fault until the next redirect
(xstop): the words after the fault can only be wanted after the faulted
ones, whose fault comes first.

core_fetchnext_tb (sim/programs/m15_fetch_next.S) replays it: a fault on
page $16, a handler that makes $16 and $17 resident and flushes $16's
entry only, then code across into $17. Two faults before, one now. make
check passes: ucode-check, lint, audit, the 18 testbenches, timing.

A sign-off is needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
